    "problem": "Let $I=Z$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i-1,\\,i\\},$ then ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=$",
    "worked_solution": "The intersection of a family of indexed sets $\\{{A}_{i}\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for all }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=-1,$ we have ${X}_{-1}=\\{-2,-1\\}$\n\n- if $i=0,$ we have ${X}_{0}=\\{-1,0\\}$\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${X}_{1}=\\{0,1\\}$\n\nand so on. \n\nNotice that there is no element that belongs to all the sets. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}=∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
